$$
\begin{aligned}
& x^{2}+y^{2}-2 z^{2}=2 a^{2} \\
& x+y+2 z=4\left(a^{2}+1\right) \\
& z^{2}-x y=a^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Given an isosceles triangle \( A B C \) where \( A B = A C \) and \( \angle A B C = 53^\circ \). Point \( K \) is such that \( C \) is the midpoint of \( A K \). Point \( M \) is chosen such that:

- \( B \) and \( M \) are on the same side of the line \( A C \);
- \( K M = A B \);
- the angle \( \angle M A K \) is the largest possible.

What is the measure of the angle \( \angle B A M \) in degrees?

Let $p$ be a prime number and $k \leq n$ be two natural numbers. We consider the base $p$ decompositions of $n$ and $k$:

$$
\begin{array}{r}
\mathrm{n}=\mathrm{n}_{\mathrm{d}} p^{\mathrm{d}}+\cdots+\mathrm{n}_{1} p+\mathrm{n}_{0} \\
\mathrm{k}=\mathrm{k}_{\mathrm{d}} p^{\mathrm{d}}+\cdots+\mathrm{k}_{1} p+\mathrm{k}_{0}
\end{array}
$$

$k_{d}$ might be 0. Show that $\binom{n}{k} \equiv\binom{n_{d}}{k_{d}} \cdots\binom{n_{0}}{k_{0}}$ mod $p$.

